import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="Security" activeNav="/security">
  <h1>Security</h1>
  <p>
    App Manager protects secrets with workspace isolation, role and API-scope checks, encryption at
    rest, explicit reveal permissions, and an audit trail. The deployment operator is still
    responsible for TLS, the host and database, backups, and the keys that make encrypted data
    recoverable.
  </p>

  <h2>What is stored, and how</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Material</th><th>Storage</th><th>Can it be retrieved?</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Local credential fields and files</td>
        <td>Envelope-encrypted with a data encryption key (DEK); the DEK is encrypted by <code>APP_KEY</code></td>
        <td>Only through an authorized reveal or download operation</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Integration, notification, OAuth, Sentry, and webhook secrets</td>
        <td>Encrypted with the application encryption keys</td>
        <td>Used by the platform; normally not returned after setup</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>API keys and invitation tokens</td>
        <td>One-way hash; the plaintext is shown only when issued</td>
        <td>No. Replace and revoke if the plaintext is lost</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>External-vault credentials</td>
        <td>Provider, resource reference, and optional metadata only</td>
        <td>The build runner resolves the secret from your vault</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Names, types, expiry dates, provider names, resource references, and other operational metadata
    are not secret payloads. They remain access-controlled, but do not put passwords or tokens into
    a name, resource reference, tag, or metadata field.
  </p>

  <h2>Envelope encryption for credentials</h2>
  <p>
    App Manager generates a 256-bit DEK and stores it in the <code>encryption_keys</code> table after
    wrapping it with <code>APP_KEY</code>, also called the key encryption key (KEK). The DEK encrypts
    credential payloads and uploaded signing files. Ciphertext records carry the DEK id used to
    create them, so data remains readable after that DEK is retired.
  </p>
  <pre><code>APP_KEY (KEK) ──encrypts──&gt; DEK in PostgreSQL
                              │
                              └──encrypts──&gt; credential fields and files</code></pre>
  <p>
    This separation makes an <code>APP_KEY</code> rotation small: the platform re-wraps the DEKs
    instead of rewriting every credential and file. It also means a database or media backup is not
    sufficient on its own — recovery needs the matching <code>APP_KEY</code> as well.
  </p>

  <h2>Access to secret material</h2>
  <p>
    Every resource belongs to one workspace. Console access is checked against the member's role;
    API access must pass both the member's role and the API key's scopes. A key with a broad scope
    cannot exceed the role of the member that owns it.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Listing credentials needs read access, but reading their contents additionally needs
      <code>credentials:reveal</code> and the <code>manage_credentials</code> permission.
    </li>
    <li>
      Reveals are recorded as access events. Secret values are excluded from audit entries, so the
      audit log does not become a second secret store.
    </li>
    <li>
      Credential downloads use private, no-store responses. Temporary plaintext files used by the
      platform are mode <code>0600</code> and removed after use.
    </li>
    <li>
      Sensitive endpoints are rate-limited. This limits abuse; it does not replace least-privilege
      roles and scopes.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Instances that should never move credential material through the REST API can set
    <code>CREDENTIALS_API_PROFILE=dashboard_only</code>. To keep all credential secrets outside App
    Manager, set <code>CREDENTIAL_STORAGE_MODES=external</code>. See
    <a href="/deployment-modes">Deployment modes</a> for the exact boundary of each mode.
  </p>

  <h2>Rotate the secret, the encryption key, or both?</h2>
  <p>
    These operations solve different problems. Rotating a DEK or <code>APP_KEY</code> changes how a
    stored value is protected; it does not change the credential, API token, or webhook secret that
    another system accepts. After suspected disclosure, rotate the exposed secret itself.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>What changed</th><th>Use this procedure</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>A signing credential or secure file</td><td>Replace its payload or upload a new file, update consumers, then retire the old material at its issuer</td></tr>
      <tr><td>An API key</td><td>Issue a replacement with minimum scopes, deploy it, confirm use, then revoke the old key</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A webhook signing secret</td><td>Rotate it from the console or CLI and update the receiver immediately; the old secret stops signing new deliveries at once</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A secret in an external vault</td><td>Rotate it in that vault; keep the App Manager reference unchanged when it points to a stable alias or <code>latest</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>The active credential DEK</td><td>Run <code>encryption:rotate-dek</code>; new writes use the new DEK and old ciphertext stays on its retired DEK</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>APP_KEY</code></td><td>Re-wrap every DEK, deploy the new key, and retain the old key temporarily in <code>APP_PREVIOUS_KEYS</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Rotating the credential DEK</h2>
  <p>
    DEK rotation does not rewrite existing data, so the operation is quick. Run it in a quiet window
    without concurrent credential writes, check the key inventory, create a new active key, and
    check it again:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`docker compose exec -T app php artisan encryption:status
docker compose exec -T app php artisan encryption:rotate-dek --force
docker compose exec -T app php artisan encryption:status`} />
  <p>
    The previous active key becomes <code>retired</code>, not deleted. New and updated credential
    payloads and newly uploaded files use the new active DEK; existing v2 ciphertext remains on the
    DEK named in that record and stays readable.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Do not delete retired DEKs while any data refers to them.</strong>
    <code>encryption:migrate</code> creates the initial key and converts legacy credential payloads
    and files to envelope encryption; it does not bulk-rewrite already-v2 ciphertext from a retired
    DEK to the current DEK.
  </p>

  <h2>Rotating APP_KEY</h2>
  <p>
    Treat <code>APP_KEY</code> rotation as a maintenance operation. It wraps every credential DEK,
    and it also protects sessions and platform secrets that use application-level encryption
    directly. A partial or uncoordinated rotation can make stored data unreadable.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Stop incoming writes and queue workers. Take and verify a database and private-media backup.</li>
    <li>Keep the current and new key in your secret manager. Generate the new value with <code>php artisan key:generate --show</code> on a trusted host.</li>
    <li>While the deployed configuration still has the old key, re-wrap all DEKs:</li>
  </ol>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`docker compose exec -T app php artisan encryption:rotate-kek \\
  --old-key="$OLD_APP_KEY" \\
  --new-key="$NEW_APP_KEY"`} />
  <ol start="4">
    <li>Set <code>APP_KEY</code> to the new value and add the old value to the comma-separated <code>APP_PREVIOUS_KEYS</code> list.</li>
    <li>Recreate every application, queue, and scheduler container so all processes load the same key set.</li>
    <li>Run <code>encryption:status</code>, reveal a test credential, exercise each configured integration, and verify that existing sessions behave as expected.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The rotation command re-wraps credential DEKs only. Directly encrypted integration fields are
    still readable through <code>APP_PREVIOUS_KEYS</code> and are written with the new key the next
    time they are replaced. Keep the previous key until those integrations have been reconnected or
    their secrets rewritten and tested. There is no generic command that proves every directly
    encrypted field has been rewritten.
  </p>
  <p>
    The key values are command arguments during the re-wrap operation. Run it only from a trusted
    administrative host, prevent command tracing and log capture, and clear any shell history that
    recorded literal values. If the command fails partway through, keep services stopped and restore
    the verified database backup before retrying.
  </p>

  <h2>Rotating application-facing secrets</h2>
  <h3>API keys</h3>
  <p>
    API keys have no in-place rotation because App Manager stores only their hashes. Create a second
    key with the same or narrower scopes, deploy it to the client, make one successful request, and
    confirm its <strong>Last used</strong> timestamp before revoking the old key. Requests using the
    revoked key fail immediately. See <a href="/platform/api-keys">API keys</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Webhook signing secrets</h3>
  <p>
    A webhook rotation returns the new HMAC secret once and starts using it immediately. Receivers
    that need a zero-loss transition should temporarily accept both the old and new values, install
    the new value as soon as rotation returns, then remove the old value after in-flight deliveries
    have cleared. Failed deliveries can be retried after the receiver is updated. See
    <a href="/platform/integrations/webhooks">Webhooks</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>External vaults</h3>
  <p>
    Prefer a stable resource reference whose active version can change inside the vault. Rotate the
    value there, make the build runner resolve it, and report the result with
    <a href="/cli/credentials"><code>appmctl credentials verify</code></a>. A successful report says
    what that runner just observed; it is not continuous monitoring and does not expire by itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Transport, logs, and backups</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Terminate TLS before production traffic and set <code>SESSION_SECURE_COOKIE=true</code>.</li>
    <li>Never pass credential payloads as CLI arguments. Use <code>--data-file</code> and redact CI logs.</li>
    <li>Restrict PostgreSQL, Redis, private media, backups, and the administrative shell to trusted networks and identities.</li>
    <li>Back up PostgreSQL, private media, the current <code>APP_KEY</code>, and every still-needed previous key as one recoverable set.</li>
    <li>Test restores. A successful database dump does not prove that its credential files and encryption keys match.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Deployment hardening, TLS, trusted proxies, and backup commands are covered in
    <a href="/self-hosting">Self-hosting</a>. Security-relevant changes and credential reveals are
    covered in the <a href="/platform/audit-log">Audit log</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>If a secret may be compromised</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Restrict or stop the affected client without deleting evidence.</li>
    <li>Issue replacement secret material and move consumers to it.</li>
    <li>Revoke the old API key or provider credential; rotate the webhook secret immediately.</li>
    <li>Review the audit log, API-key last-use times, webhook deliveries, and provider-side access logs.</li>
    <li>Rotate the DEK or <code>APP_KEY</code> only when the encryption layer may also be exposed; doing so alone does not invalidate a leaked application secret.</li>
  </ol>
</DocsLayout>
